BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602p@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T100200
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T102400
SUMMARY:Enable runtime modification of nohz_full and managed_irq housekeepi
 ng CPUs
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Waiman Long · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2568/\n\nBy using the cpuset isolated partition fu
 nctionality in the Linux\nkernel\, users are now able to change the set of
  "isolcpus[=domain]"\nHK_TYPE_DOMAIN housekeeping CPUs at runtime. This fe
 ature is used by\nsome Kubernetes based container orchestration platforms 
 to enable the\ncreation of containers running latency sensitive workloads 
 like DPDK.\n\nDomain isolation by itself doesn't provide enough CPU isolat
 ion\, so the\nsystem has to be booted with a set of boot-time enabled nohz
 _full and\nmanaged_irq isolated CPUs which are then combined with runtime 
 domain\nisolation to create the desired isolated CPUs for workloads that n
 eed\nthem. There are some wasted system overhead to have boot-time isolate
 d\nnohz_full and managed_irq CPUs that are not actually being used in\niso
 lated cpuset partitons.\n\nEventually we would like to have nohz_full and 
 managed_irq isolated\nCPUs created at runtime when they are needed. This t
 alk is about the\nprogress we have made in this direction and additional f
 uture works\nthat are needed to achieve this goal.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2568/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602q@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T102400
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T104600
SUMMARY:Bringing Proxy Execution’s benefits to Userland / Futexes
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · John Stultz\, Suleiman <> Souhlal
  · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2569/\n\nMore and more we’
 re seeing issues around priority (sometimes called performance) inversion 
 of SCHED_NORMAL/BATCH tasks. Particularly if any sort of constraints are p
 ut on “background” deprioritized tasks. These background tasks will ev
 entually grab an important lock\, and then won’t be constrained and prev
 ented from running for some extended period of time\, resulting in all the
  important tasks becoming blocked waiting for them to release the lock. Si
 nce the tasks are fair tasks\, the delays are not indefinite\, but they st
 ill can be substantial and user visible.\n\nPI Futexes seem like a good so
 lution here\, but the underlying rt_mutex behavior doesn’t help SCHED_NO
 RMAL tasks as rt_mutex priority inheritance isn’t used between SCHED_NOR
 MAL tasks since ~v5.10 or so. Further\, even on systems where kernels patc
 h rt_mutexes do “nice inheritance” (as imperfect as that is) for NORMA
 L tasks\, the strict rt_mutex handoff behavior results in pi-futex not per
 forming as well as normal futexes.\n\nThus\, there is a desire to preserve
  the performance characteristics of normal futexes\, while also providing 
 priority inheritance to avoid priority/performance inversions.\n\nProxy Ex
 ecution has been a feature in development for many years now\, which provi
 des semantics similar to what is desired in this case. Proxy Execution wor
 ks for in-kernel mutexes (and rw_sems)\, and provides priority-inheritance
  without the strict handoff ordering and performance overhead that rt_mute
 xes can cause.  For RT tasks\, rt_mutexes and their strict behavior is sti
 ll important\, but for other sched-classes Proxy Execution provides much o
 f the benefits without the costs.\n\nSo it would be nice to similarly enab
 le futexes to benefit from Proxy Execution’s generalized form of priorit
 y inheritance and avoid the negative performance impact of PI futexes.\n\n
 Unfortunately\, the existing normal futex API is insufficient to be used w
 ith Proxy Execution\, as when a task is blocked on a futex lock\, Proxy Ex
 ecution has to understand who the owner of that lock is\, so they can be r
 un to release the needed lock. Thus we need to find a way to extend the no
 rmal futex API so that the lock owner is communicated to the kernel.\n\nPr
 oxy Execution is not yet fully upstream\, so this discussion is a little p
 remature\, but since uAPI is important to get right\, we wanted to start t
 he discussion early so we can plan and work toward an acceptable solution.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2569/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602r@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T104600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T110800
SUMMARY:PREEMPT_RT: How a simple interface up event might break your realti
 me
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Florian Bezdeka · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2575/\n\nRealtimers know: Resource configurati
 on for RT enabled systems is key. RT applications rely on a proper system 
 configuration and expect this configuration to stay unmodified as long as 
 the application is alive.\n\nAs it turned out\, Linux might silently recon
 figure IRQ affinities of network adapters in a way that violates the expec
 ted system configuration. While we run into this kind of problem in the ar
 ea of networking first\, the\nproblem seems generic to all kind of "multi 
 queue devices".\n\nWe ended up having two problematic situations:\n\n- RT 
 applications (partially) breaking out of their previously requested system
  configuration as Linux ignores previously requested affinities during rec
 onfiguration\n- non RT applications polluting isolated (RT) CPU cores via 
 IRQs\n\nAnd there is even more flexibility on the horizon already: The pro
 per system\nconfiguration might not be known at boot time. Using container
  runtimes for the deployment of RT applications requires an adaptive resou
 rce management that might change IRQ affinities at runtime.\n\nIn this ses
 sion we will showcase a couple of shortcomings related to resource managem
 ent in the networking area. Starting with how network drivers are\nviolati
 ng CPU isolation by implementing IRQ spreading\, we want to discuss\n\n - 
 How do we close the gap between cgroups (cpuset controller) and the IRQ co
 re?\n - How should we adjust all the affected drivers to honor the cgroup 
 configuration?\n - What APIs are necessary to get there?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2575/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2636@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T110800
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T113000
SUMMARY:Understanding differences in results between timerlat and cyclictes
 t
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Tomas Glozar\, John Kacur\, Luis 
 Goncalves · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2636/\n\nThe propos
 al is to discuss differences in results between timerlat and cyclictest an
 d the reasons behind that. Once the reasons are understood\, ways to reduc
 e the differences could be sought. In addition\, the discussion could incl
 ude rtla feature parity with rt-tests\, as well as between different rtla 
 tools (osnoise\, timerlat)\, and integrating rtla with other tools such as
  perf.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2636/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602s@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T122200
SUMMARY:Remaining EEVDF scheduling latency and lag improvement
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Vincent Guittot · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2570/\n\nRecent improvements have been made in
  tasks ordering\, scheduling latency and lag of the fair/EEVDF scheduler b
 ut some issues still remain and will involve more complex mechanisms. The 
 talk will discuss possible solutions for a number of open issues:\nHow to 
 fix the remaining out of range lag of tasks ?\nHow to further decrease the
  scheduling latency ?\nHow to select the best CPU to minimize scheduling l
 atency?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2570/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2574@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T122200
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T124400
SUMMARY:Kernel Sched QoS Interface
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Qais Yousef · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2571/\n\nFollowing last year discussion about user
 space assissted scheduling [1]\, schedqos utility is announced [2] and a p
 roposal for kernel interface to help address one of the QoS issues folks s
 ee in the wild\, DVFS and migration latencies\, was sent also [3].\n\nThe 
 interface proposal is a simple extension to sched_attr\, but has the goal 
 of being extensible without requiring further addition to sched_attr.\n\nS
 ome of the key areas to discuss:\n\n1. Deprecatability: We want to end up 
 with baggage ABI we don't want to carry forward if internals change and a 
 QoS no longer make sense.\n2. Extensibility: We want to allow almost arbit
 rary description of behavior without having to modify the interface.\n3. D
 iscoverability: how can users know a set of QoS are available?\n\nCurrentl
 y there are two proposed users\, rampup_multiplier to manage DVFS response
  time\, and tag memory dependency between tasks for cache aware scheduling
 .\n\nThe latter had interesting discussions about cookie management and wh
 ether ownership should be in kernel space or userspace.\n\nWe will go thro
 ugh all open questions and hope to come up with a plan of what to do next.
 \n\n[1] https://lpc.events/event/19/contributions/2089/\n[2] https://lore.
 kernel.org/lkml/20260415000910.2h5misvwc45bdumu@airbuntu/\n[3] https://lor
 e.kernel.org/lkml/20260504020003.71306-9-qyousef@layalina.io/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2571/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602t@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T124400
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T130600
SUMMARY:Energy-Aware Scheduling on x86 Hybrid Topologies: Latency Analysis 
 and Idle CPU Selection
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Ricardo Neri · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2574/\n\nDuring OSPM 2026\, there was an agreemen
 t to stop requiring schedutil to enable EAS on x86 platforms\, which impli
 es EAS may soon be active by default on these systems.\n\nEnergy-Aware Sch
 eduling (EAS) places tasks by comparing estimated energy costs across perf
 ormance domains\, directing light tasks to power-efficient cores on asymme
 tric systems. The known trade-off is that EAS packing introduces runqueue 
 contention when idle CPUs are available: tasks are packed onto fewer CPUs\
 , and wakeups that land on already-busy CPUs incur queuing delay even when
  other CPUs sit idle.\n\nThis talk uses quantitative wakeup latency decomp
 osition on x86 hybrid platforms as a starting point to motivate making EAS
  more aggressive in selecting idle CPUs. I examine where in find_energy_ef
 ficient_cpu() idle-CPU preference can be strengthened without abandoning t
 he energy-efficiency objective\, measure the resulting impact on tail late
 ncy (p99+) and energy consumption via platform power counters\, and discus
 s how the latency–power trade-off compares to the packing baseline.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2574/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602u@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T153727Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T130600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T132800
SUMMARY:sparsemask: the missing generic plumbing and next step forward
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Scheduler and Real-Time MC · Prateek Nayak · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2573/\n\nAs CPU counts grow\, Linux scheduler sc
 alability suffers from contention on global cpumasks — frequent atomic u
 pdates to shared cachelines become a measurable bottleneck on large core-c
 ount systems.\n\nTwo proposals address this: Steve Sistare's sparsmask\, w
 hich distributes a cpumask across multiple cachelines to reduce contention
  [1]\, and Peter Zijlstra's sbm (sparse bitmap) [2]\, a simpler\, topology
 -aware evolution of the same idea. While sbm effectively eliminates cachel
 ine ping-pong\, its generic infrastructure falls short in one critical are
 a: CPU hotplug handling\, where topological information for offline CPUs m
 ay be unavailable.\n\nThis talk covers:\n\n - **The gap**: shortcomings in
  the generic sbm layer for hotplug scenarios\n - **Peter's proposal for x8
 6** to address the challenges with offline CPUs\, and what's still needed 
 to make sparsemask truly generic\n - **A scheduler-native alternative**: a
 n orthogonal sbm implementation scoped to the scheduler\, leveraging hotpl
 ug callbacks to dynamically size sbm allocations.\n\nPrevious version of t
 his work was posted at [3] and was discussed at LPC2025.\n\nReferences:\n\
 n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/1541767840-93588-2-git-send-email-steven
 .sistare@oracle.com/\n[2] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260324120008.GB37
 38010@noisy.programming.kicks-ass.net/\n[3] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/2
 0251208083602.31898-1-kprateek.nayak@amd.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2573/
END:VEVENT
